    title='Early insulin delivery and fixed-dose design trade-offs in a proposed human-scale islet implant'
    h('Abstract')
    p(f'''Insulin production and delivery across an implant boundary are distinct engineering endpoints. We calculate the transfer response of the proposed seven-pouch BEAM human-scale geometry and its fixed-dose design requirements. Uniform production maintained for 30 minutes in 0.75 mm annuli gives {100*e:.2f}% delivery with assumed diffusivity 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m²/s and perfect outer/end sinks. Continuum and finite-volume calculations agree to about 0.0012 percentage points. A nominal 0.60 mm redesign reaches 80.21% but falls below 80% with reflecting ends or a 5% diffusivity reduction. Reoptimized 80% choices require 141.18 cm² lateral area under favorable assumptions, 240.09 cm² with lower diffusivity alone, 289.12 cm² with finite outer permeability alone, and 381.79 cm² with both changes and reflecting ends. A declared 24-scenario set selects the same 0.21 mm design. If a hypothetical minimum thickness of 0.25 or 0.30 mm is imposed, the highest worst-scenario delivery on the remaining grid is only 75.90% or 69.67%; the 80% target becomes unattainable within that constrained family. These assumed constraints do not identify a biological thickness limit. The device-specific map distinguishes additional area requirements from loss of target attainability and identifies the transport and packing measurements needed to choose among scenarios.''')
    h('1 Introduction')
    p('''Islet encapsulation must preserve viable insulin-secreting cells while permitting timely transport. Pham and colleagues reported the continuously oxygenated macroencapsulation platform BEAM and proposed a seven-pouch human-scale configuration [1]. The proposal supplies dimensions and a dose target, but its human-scale drawing is not a human efficacy experiment. Its hour-long static secretion assays also do not directly determine how a new production increment crosses the proposed geometry during the first 30 minutes.''')
    p('''Secretion–transport coupling, geometry-dependent delay and pressure-driven improvement are established [2–5]. For the later BEAM proposal, the present map connects a reconciled dose and geometry to actual finite annular segments, timed boundary delivery and the costs of redesign. It lets a reader determine which assumed transport conditions require more area and whether a specified minimum layer thickness removes every target-meeting design from the sampled family. Table 1 distinguishes this device-specific decision from the earlier general transport and geometry results.''')
    table('Table 1. Closest prior work and the specific added output here.', ['Study / geometry','Source input','Endpoint','Experimental basis / validation','Additional output here'],[
      ['Buchwald [2]; encapsulated islets','Glucose-driven secretion','Dynamic insulin release','Dynamic release experiments and coupled model','Later BEAM proposal; explicit fixed-dose segments'],
      ['Ernst [3]; SHARP device designs','Islet size/position and oxygen-consumption inputs','Oxygen-limited functional capacity','Computational platform with experimental comparisons','Uniform-source timing isolated from oxygen function'],
      ['Thomson [4]; macroencapsulation chambers','Stimulus-linked production / transport','Insulin delivery dynamics','Pressure-driven transport and device experiments','Passive finite-annulus map at stated BEAM dose'],
      ['Trask [5]; reservoir, membrane and tissue','Initial insulin bolus','External transport; fibrosis and flow','Measured tracer membrane inputs; no direct experimental device-response validation','Continuous-input convolution and fixed-dose BEAM design map']])
    h('2 Geometry, parameters and measurement boundaries')
    p('''An islet equivalent (IEQ) is the conventional volume of a 150 µm diameter spherical islet; it is not a count of physically identical spheres. Let a and b be inner and outer annular radii, h = b − a the layer thickness, L the active segment length, N the total IEQ dose, ρ the effective IEQ loading density, and s₀ the uniform incremental production per volume and time. Nominally there are seven segments with a = 3.00 mm, b = 3.75 mm, h = 0.75 mm and L = 70 mm. Straight annular ends and homogeneous loading idealize the rounded proposal; they do not reconstruct device CAD or actual cell positions (Figure 1).''')
    table('Table 2. Parameter and assumption provenance.', ['Quantity','Value used','Provenance class','Qualification'],[
      ['Outer diameter; layer; length; count','7.5 mm; 0.75 mm; 70 mm; 7','Reported proposal [1]','Supplementary Fig.24, p.21; not measured as-built geometry'],
      ['Dose N','420,000 IEQ','Reported target [1]','Held fixed in every redesign'],
      ['Active volume; effective ρ','7.7931 mL; 53,893.7 IEQ/mL','Derived','Straight annuli; dose/volume'],
      ['Nominal D','3.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m²/s','Assumed scenario','No measured BEAM-gel value; not a universal maximum'],
      ['D sensitivity','1.0, 2.0, 2.85, 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m²/s','Assumed scenarios','Selected sensitivity points; no distribution or empirical coverage'],
      ['Outer permeability P','10⁻⁶, 10⁻⁵ m/s; perfect sink','Assumed scenarios','No fitted assembled-device values'],
      ['Ends; source; receiving medium','Absorbing/reflecting; uniform; zero background increment','Structural assumptions','No tissue transport, heterogeneous secretion or feedback'],
      ['Transport calibration in this geometry','None','Not measured','Literature measurements belong to other systems']])
    p('''The straight-annulus volume at the separate reported 60,000 IEQ/mL would imply 467,586.8 IEQ. Even the rounded caption arithmetic, 7 × 1.11 mL × 60,000 IEQ/mL, gives 466,200 IEQ rather than 420,000 IEQ. We preserve the stated dose by recalculating effective density. Unfilled volume, rounded ends or other details could matter, but no explanation is assigned without evidence. This discrepancy rescales capacity and oxygen accounting; it does not change a normalized linear delivery fraction with a fixed spatial source.''')
    fig('geometry_schematic.png','Original model schematic. A: the shaded annulus produces insulin; the inner boundary reflects. B: the outer boundary is absorbing or Robin, and both ends are absorbing or reflecting. C: dose-preserving redesigns use full 70 mm segments and one remainder; the illustration does not specify a particular pouch count. The model excludes connectors and the oxygen generator.')
    h('3 Transfer response and numerical methods')
    p('''For one segment, Ω is a ≤ r ≤ b, 0 ≤ z ≤ L with rotational symmetry. Incremental concentration c is zero initially and obeys diffusion plus a spatially uniform source q(t)/V, where q is the total production rate and V the corresponding modeled volume. The inner wall has zero normal derivative. At the outer wall, the outward flux is −D∂c/∂n = Pc; a perfect sink is the limit c = 0. The end faces are either c = 0 or ∂c/∂n = 0. Delivered mass counts the integrated outward flux on all accessible boundaries, not systemic insulin exposure.''')
    eq(r'\partial_t c=D\left[r^{-1}\partial_r(r\partial_r c)+\partial_z^2c\right]+q(t)/V')
    eq(r'M_{\mathrm{out}}(T)=\int_0^T\!\int_{\partial\Omega}(-D\nabla c\cdot\mathbf{n})\,dA\,dt')
    p('''Let S(t) be the remaining fraction after a uniformly distributed unit initial inventory evolves under the source-free transport operator. For zero initial incremental inventory, linearity gives the general cumulative response. A nonzero uniform initial amount M₀ would contribute M₀[1 − S(T)] separately.''')
    eq(r'M_{\mathrm{out}}(T)=\int_0^T q(u)\,[1-S(T-u)]\,du')
    p('''Two observables are kept distinct. The continuous-source deadline fraction η(T) uses constant q = s₀V until the chosen deadline T. The fixed-pulse recovery Rτ(t) uses q = s₀V only for 0 < t ≤ τ, with τ = 30 minutes, and normalizes by the fixed amount s₀Vτ. Before pulse completion, Rτ counts recovery relative to that full eventual pulse; after shutoff its denominator stays unchanged. They coincide at T = t = τ.''')
    eq(r'\eta(T)=1-\frac{1}{T}\int_0^T S(v)\,dv,\qquad R_\tau(t)=\frac{M_{\mathrm{out}}(t)}{s_0V\tau}')
    p('''The continuum implementation uses Bessel radial modes and odd sine axial modes for absorbing ends; reflecting ends retain only the zero axial mode. For a uniform source, the self-adjoint diffusion operator gives nonnegative modal weights. Radial Robin roots satisfy Dk f₁(b) = Pf₀(b), where f₀(r) = J₀(kr)Y₁(ka) − Y₀(kr)J₁(ka) and f₁ = −f₀′/k. Perfect outer sinks instead require f₀(b) = 0. Survival is the sum of modal weights times their exponential decay. Actual segment responses are weighted by volume.''')
    p('''An independently constructed conservative finite-volume spatial operator uses cylindrical volumes and face conductances, including the outer resistance. Exact-time eigendecomposition avoids time-step error. Refinement, quadrature checks on continuum weights, positivity checks and independent face-flux integration test implementation and conservation. The primary continuum/FV difference is 0.00118 percentage points; the estimated primary numerical uncertainty is 0.00322 percentage points. Positive omitted-mode tails bound truncation, but numerical roots and floating-point guards do not form certified interval arithmetic. Full formulas and numerical values are in METHODS.md and the evidence files.''')
    p('''For the fixed perfect-sink geometry, changing D scales the diffusion clock; lower D cannot increase delivery. Adding passive resistance or reflecting a formerly absorbing face cannot advance first exit for the same source. These are conditional ordering statements. The simple clock-scaling proof does not apply unchanged at fixed finite P, because P/D changes. The Robin design map therefore evaluates every declared combination and claims robustness only across those discrete scenarios.''')
    h('4 Delivery, waveform and the nominal design')
    p(f'''The nominal continuous-source calculation gives η(30 min) = {100*e:.2f}%, below the prespecified 80% engineering benchmark. The benchmark has no demonstrated clinical acceptance meaning. At shutoff, {100*(1-e):.2f}% remains in the implant; almost all of the fixed pulse has left by 150 minutes (Figure 2). For comparison, a uniform initial inventory gives {100*rv['waveform']['instant_uniform_initial_inventory_fraction_delivered_by30min']:.2f}% recovery by 30 minutes. The two inputs can have equal total amount but have different molecule ages and peak-rate constraints; an ideal instantaneous bolus is not a demonstrated cellular secretion waveform.''')
    p('''The nominal diffusion scale h²/D is 31.25 minutes and DT/h² is 0.96 at 30 minutes. The deadline is therefore comparable with transport across the layer; this scale is not an identical exit time for every molecule. Moving production into the outer half or quarter of the same domain raises the modeled fraction to 82.05% or 90.07%, with local density multipliers 1.895 or 3.692. These source-placement cases explicitly rule out a placement-independent 72% ceiling. They do not establish feasible cell packing or local oxygen supply.''')
    fig('production_delivery.png','Response to a fixed 30-minute production increment. A enlarges the first 60 minutes and marks shutoff; output is normalized to the fixed on-state production rate s₀V. B follows cumulative production, boundary delivery and retained inventory to 150 minutes, each normalized to total pulse production. The recovery curve Rτ differs from η(T) for a source maintained to every deadline.')
    p('''The redesign family preserves N = 420,000 IEQ, ρ and b while varying h from 0.10 to 1.00 mm in 0.01 mm steps. Total length follows the fixed volume, and the minimum number of segments of at most 70 mm is used, with one shorter remainder. This prevents cost-free proliferation of absorbing end faces. In the SI length formula below, ρ = 5.38937 × 10¹⁰ IEQ/m³ and radii are in metres.''')
    eq(r'L_{\mathrm{tot}}(h)=\frac{N}{\rho\pi[b^2-(b-h)^2]},\qquad A_{\mathrm{lat}}=2\pi bL_{\mathrm{tot}}')
