    table('Table 4. Fragility of the nominal 80% choice. Each geometry obeys the dose-preserving rule.', ['Case','30-minute delivery','Benchmark result'],[[label,f"{100*float(r['eta30']):.4f}%",'Above' if float(r['eta30'])>=.8 else 'Below'] for label,r in zip(case_names,checks[:5])])
    p(f'''The critical diffusivity for the 0.60 mm absorbing-end design to retain 80% delivery is approximately {rv['critical_D_h060_m2_s']/1e-10:.4f} × 10⁻¹⁰ m²/s, only 1.1% below the assumed nominal value. Reflecting ends yield 79.9934%; refined continuum and finite-volume results place this below the threshold rather than a rounding ambiguity. The 0.61 mm case changes total length and segmentation to preserve dose, so it is a design-family comparison, not a measured manufacturing tolerance. The original 22.28% area increment is therefore an idealized grid requirement, not a robust recommendation.''')
    p('''We evaluate all 24 combinations of D = {1.0, 2.0, 2.85, 3.0} × 10⁻¹⁰ m²/s, P = {10⁻⁶, 10⁻⁵ m/s, perfect sink}, and absorbing or reflecting ends, denoted S24. Subset S8 uses D = {2.85, 3.0} × 10⁻¹⁰ and P = {10⁻⁵ m/s, perfect sink}, with both end conditions. F denotes the favorable single scenario. These finite sets are assumed sensitivity cases, with no measured parameter coverage or assigned probabilities. The 91 thicknesses and three deadlines produce 6,552 results.''')
    selected=[r for r in targets if int(r['deadline_min'])==30 and float(r['target_eta'])==.8]
    table('Table 5. Minimum-area sampled 80% designs at 30 minutes. Limiting combinations (D in 10⁻¹⁰ m²/s; P in m/s): F, D = 3, P = ∞, absorbing ends; S8, D = 2.85, P = 10⁻⁵, reflecting ends; S24, D = 1, P = 10⁻⁶, reflecting ends. Area increase is relative to the original 0.75 mm geometry.', ['Set','h mm','Worst delivery','Area cm²','Increase %','Pouches','Footprint cm²'],[[label,f"{float(r['thickness_mm']):.2f}",f"{100*float(r['minimum_eta']):.2f}%",f"{float(r['lateral_area_cm2']):.2f}",f"{100*(float(r['area_ratio_to_nominal'])-1):.2f}",r['pouch_count'],f"{float(r['active_bounding_footprint_cm2']):.2f}"] for label,r in zip(['F','S8','S24'],selected)])
    p('''A design qualifies when its lower numerical estimate meets the target for every set member. Every lower-area competitor must have a scenario whose upper estimate is below target; unresolved overlaps trigger refinement. The accepted map satisfies this rule and includes 70 representative, selected-worst and adjacent finite-volume checks. The original near-threshold correction is retained in the supplementary record.''')
    p('''The limiting combinations are shown with the selected designs in Table 5 and Figure 4. All scenario members were evaluated; the observed worst cases do not certify untested parameter values. The nominal 50% target at 30 minutes already passes at the thickest tested layer, so that minimum is limited by the grid boundary.''')
    fig('design_constraints.png','A: minimum delivery across each declared set, with the selected 80% designs labeled. B: maximum worst-scenario delivery over all remaining S24 designs satisfying the hypothetical minimum thickness. The 0.25 and 0.30 mm constraints remove every 80% solution on this grid. Set definitions are printed in the figure; no empirical minimum thickness is assigned.',6.5)
    h('6 Minimum thickness and transport mechanisms')
    p('''The S24 80% choice has a 210 µm layer, only 1.4 times the IEQ reference diameter; its 90% choice is 120 µm. Neither ratio decides whether an actual preparation can be loaded. Islet-size distribution, deformation, placement and clearance must determine an allowable layer thickness. We therefore leave the minimum permitted thickness as an unassigned parameter.''')
    eq(r'\mathcal{H}(h_{\min})=\{h\in\mathcal{G}:h\geq h_{\min}\},\qquad E_{\max}(h_{\min})=\max_{h\in\mathcal{H}(h_{\min})}\min_{s\in\mathcal{S}}\eta_s(30\,\mathrm{min};h)')
    p('''Here G is the original 0.10–1.00 mm grid, and S is a declared scenario set. Every remaining thickness is enumerated, retaining fixed dose, density, outer radius and segmentation. Maximum-delivery estimates use the maxima of the inherited lower and upper numerical estimates; the minimum-area selection uses the same threshold and every-competitor checks as before. The analysis spans 91 minimum-thickness values in all three sets, giving 273 maxima and 546 decisions for 80% and 90% targets. No additional transport simulation is required.''')
    floor_rows=[]
    for floor in [.10,.21,.25,.30]:
        m=next(r for r in maxima if r['scenario_set']=='broad_discrete' and abs(float(r['h_min_mm'])-floor)<1e-9)
