    table('Table 6. S24 at 30 minutes after a hypothetical minimum-thickness constraint. Listed h values are the minimum-area sampled choices; not attained means all remaining designs fall below the target.', ['Minimum h mm','Highest worst delivery','80% selection','90% selection'],floor_rows)
    p('''With minimum thicknesses of 0.25 or 0.30 mm, the remaining-grid maximum is 75.90% or 69.67%. Even its upper numerical estimate is below 80%. The target therefore cannot be recovered by adding area through further thinning within this constrained family. Changing radius, packing, segmentation or transport would define a different family. A minimum-thickness constraint is necessary input to this decision, but is not sufficient evidence of physical realizability.''')
    mechanism_labels=['Favorable','Lower D only','Finite P only','Reflecting ends only','All three changes']
    table('Table 7. Separate minimum-area reoptimizations for 80% at 30 minutes. Favorable: D = 3 × 10⁻¹⁰ m²/s, perfect outer sink, absorbing ends. The individual changes are D = 1 × 10⁻¹⁰, P = 10⁻⁶ m/s, or reflecting ends. All three applies them together. No minimum thickness beyond the original grid is imposed.', ['Scenario change','h mm','Area cm²','Achieved delivery'],[[label,f"{float(r['thickness_mm']):.2f}",f"{float(r['lateral_area_cm2']):.2f}",f"{100*float(r['minimum_eta']):.2f}%"] for label,r in zip(mechanism_labels,mechanisms)])
    p('''End reflection defeats the narrowly passing 0.60 mm point, yet reoptimization restores 80% at 0.59 mm with only 2.19 cm² (1.55%) more area than the favorable redesign. Lower diffusivity alone requires 98.91 cm² more, and finite permeability alone 147.94 cm² more. Threshold sensitivity and a large resource penalty are thus different design questions. These costs are nonadditive results of five separate optimizations; they do not rank mechanisms in an actual device.''')
    eq(r'\mathrm{Fo}=\frac{DT}{h^2},\qquad \mathrm{Bi}=\frac{Ph}{D}')
    p('''Fo compares the deadline with the internal diffusion time h²/D. Bi compares outer permeability with internal diffusive conductance D/h; small Bi indicates relatively strong boundary resistance, while a perfect sink has infinite Bi. For fixed D and finite P, thinning raises Fo and lowers Bi: both the internal time h²/D and boundary scale h/P improve, but boundary resistance can become relatively more important. At their selected thicknesses, the favorable and lower-D-only choices have Fo = 1.50 and 1.56; the finite-P-only choice has Fo = 6.89 and Bi = 0.93. Thus rapid internal transport does not eliminate a finite outer resistance. Annular radius ratios, segment aspect ratios and end access remain additional controls.''')
    h('7 Physical uncertainty and discriminating validation')
    p('''Numerical uncertainty concerns how accurately the chosen equations are solved. Parameter uncertainty concerns D, permeability, dimensions and production distribution within that model. Structural uncertainty concerns homogeneous diffusion, discrete islets, heterogeneous secretion, tissue transport and feedback. The nominal diffusivity change from 3.0 to 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m²/s changes delivery from 71.53% to 45.81%, far more than numerical error. Thus a narrow numerical interval is not a narrow prediction interval for an actual implant.''')
    p('''The computed response, passive ordering argument and real-device prediction are distinct levels of inference. The first two apply within their defined model; the third requires measured correspondence of geometry, source and boundaries. Boundary flux does not itself establish physiological performance. Tissue transport, heterogeneous secretion, feedback, viable packing and a spatial oxygen field remain outside this calculation.''')
    p('''A discriminating experiment would first measure insulin diffusion in the gel independently of the assembled membrane. Controlled assembled-device measurements would then vary receiving conditions and thickness to separate boundary resistance from internal diffusion. Insulin should be assayed directly; a surrogate requires a predeclared equivalence test. Recovery controls must quantify retained insulin, adsorption, degradation and assay recovery so missing mass is not automatically assigned to slow diffusion.''')
    p('''Calibrate transport parameters on one geometry, freeze them and predict at least one held-out thickness or configuration without refitting. Predeclare cumulative delivery at 5, 15, 30 and 60 minutes, time to 50% recovery, normalized curve error and mass-balance discrepancy as comparison metrics. Predictive acceptance tolerances should be fixed from independent assay precision and repeatability before held-out data are inspected; none is invented here. Early sampling plus retained-inventory measurement is essential. Profile likelihood or a sensitivity/Jacobian analysis should test whether D and P are separately identifiable. If they are correlated, report joint ambiguity and add independent material measurements or thickness/receiving-condition contrasts. Cell-bearing validation would subsequently require independently characterized secretion inputs and source locations. This is a proposed validation plan, not a performed experiment.''')
